1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,100
Climate Change: How Earth Is Warming.

2
00:00:03,050 --> 00:00:19,337
The Earth's average surface temperature has risen approximately 1.2°C above pre-industrial levels (1850–1900), according to the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change (IPCC) Sixth Assessment Report (2021).

3
00:00:19,337 --> 00:00:24,375
This warming is primarily driven by anthropogenic greenhouse gas emissions.

4
00:00:24,375 --> 00:00:38,587
Atmospheric carbon dioxide concentrations have surged from roughly 280 parts per million before the Industrial Revolution to over 420 ppm in 2023 — a level not seen in at least 2 million years.

5
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:41,737
The consequences span the entire planet.

6
00:00:41,737 --> 00:00:59,350
Global mean sea level rose by 0.20 metres between 1901 and 2018, with the rate of rise accelerating from 1.3 mm per year over the 20th century to approximately 3.7 mm per year between 2006 and 2018.

7
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:11,337
The Greenland and Antarctic ice sheets together lost an average of 430 billion tonnes of mass per year between 2006 and 2015, contributing substantially to this rise.

8
00:01:11,337 --> 00:01:24,812
Arctic sea ice extent has declined by roughly 13% per decade since 1979, and the region is warming nearly four times faster than the global average — a phenomenon known as Arctic amplification.

9
00:01:24,812 --> 00:01:28,687
Glaciers in almost every mountain range on Earth are retreating.

10
00:01:28,687 --> 00:01:46,212
The IPCC projects that under high-emission scenarios, global warming could reach 3.3 to 5.7°C by 2100, while limiting warming to 1.5°C requires reaching net-zero CO₂ emissions by approximately 2050.

11
00:01:46,212 --> 00:01:54,250
These findings form the scientific foundation of international climate policy, including the 2015 Paris Agreement.

